85392	{"artifactId":"DEF-c2ba6fa62a148ed1","entry":{"id":"migrated-DEF-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-11T05:19:54.318Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"define","stage":"define","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p1","ref":"c1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/db/relations.ts writes out-edge rows with an empty value: `s.outEdge.put(encodeOutEdgeKey(fromU64, kindByte, toU64), Buffer.alloc(0));`, so a stored relationship carries no record of how it was established.","evidence":"The quoted statement `s.outEdge.put(encodeOutEdgeKey(fromU64, kindByte, toU64), Buffer.alloc(0));` was read verbatim at all three cited anchors: src/db/relations.ts:144, src/db/relations.ts:395 and src/db/relations.ts:434. The exact-pattern grep returns only those three source hits plus the artifact's own citation line (DEF.md:349). The broader `(outEdge|inEdge).put(` grep shows the paired in-edge writes at src/db/relations.ts:145, :396 and :435 also pass `Buffer.alloc(0)`, and no production write site in the evidence stores a non-empty edge value (every other match is under `__tests__/`). So the edge rows are written with an empty value, which supports the conclusion that a stored relationship carries no record of how it was established in the edge row itself.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p2","ref":"4. Assumptions / c1","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Edge rows encode from-id, kind byte and to-id entirely in the key (encodeOutEdgeKey) and every edge write passes an empty buffer as the value, so an evidence value can be added without changing edge keys.","evidence":"The evidence confirms the premise. `src/db/graph/keys.ts:145` declares `encodeOutEdgeKey(from: bigint, kind: number, to: bigint): Buffer`, and `src/db/graph/keys.ts:156` declares the mirror `encodeInEdgeKey(to, kind, from)`, so from-id, kind byte and to-id are all carried in the key. The three production write sites in `src/db/relations.ts` (lines 144/145, 395/396 and 434/435) each call `s.outEdge.put(encodeOutEdgeKey(fromU64, kindByte, toU64), Buffer.alloc(0))` and `s.inEdge.put(encodeInEdgeKey(toU64, kindByte, fromU64), Buffer.alloc(0))`; the read of `src/db/relations.ts:144` matches verbatim. The other matches are test fixtures (store, traversal, compact, sc1-external-endpoint, entities-lmdb, reindex-file-lmdb, repo-stats) and all also pass `Buffer.alloc(0)`. One limit: the grep pattern only matches puts that already pass an empty buffer, so it shows no counter-example but cannot by itself prove \"every\" edge write is empty-valued. That does not alter the prescribed change, since the value slot is demonstrably unused at all write sites shown and the key shape is independent of the value.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p3","ref":"c2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The per-file refresh in src/indexer/index.ts is a series of separate awaited store calls, including `await deleteRelationsForFile(this.db, filePath);`, not one transaction, so a failure part-way leaves a file with no entities or with entities and no relationships.","evidence":"The cited quote is confirmed verbatim at both anchors: the read of `src/indexer/index.ts:755` returned `await deleteRelationsForFile(this.db, filePath);` and the read of `src/indexer/index.ts:1011` returned the same line. The exact-quote grep found exactly those two source sites, plus the artifact's own c2 citation at DEF.md:350. Each is a standalone awaited statement, which fits the premise's \"separate awaited store calls\". A sibling artifact (ISSUE.md:20 under `docs/standalone/index-never-reconciled-against-files-disk-E202610102764c29d`) cites `await deleteEntitiesForFile(this.db, filePath);` from the same file, which corroborates that the entity delete is a separate awaited call. Limit: the broader grep for the sibling store calls was truncated at its 50-match cap before reaching `src/indexer/index.ts`, so the evidence does not directly show the neighbouring calls or the absence of a wrapping transaction. Nothing in the evidence contradicts that part.","action":"none — verified sound. The citation and both anchors resolve to the quoted line. Optionally, re-run the sibling-call probe scoped to `src/indexer/index.ts` to show directly that no transaction wraps the calls around lines 755 and 1011.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p4","ref":"c3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/db/entities.ts exports a whole-file replacement `export async function reindexFile(`.","evidence":"The grep for /export async function reindexFile\\(/ matched src/db/entities.ts:363 (`export async function reindexFile(`), and the read of src/db/entities.ts:363 returned FOUND with the same line. The only other match is the artifact's own citation at DEF.md:351. The anchor file, line and symbol all resolve to the cited entity. The \"whole-file replacement\" description of the function's behaviour is not shown by the single-line read, but the cited export exists exactly where stated.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p5","ref":"s2/lc1, c15","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"reindexFile has 0 callers: the only occurrence of `reindexFile(` in non-test source is its own definition in src/db/entities.ts.","evidence":"The anchor is confirmed: the read of src/db/entities.ts:363 returns `export async function reindexFile(`, so the definition is where the premise says it is. The \"0 callers in non-test source\" half is not established by the evidence. The grep for /\\breindexFile\\b/ returned 50 matches and was TRUNCATED at the cap. All 50 shown matches are in docs/ (DEF.md:351, DEF.md:363, docs/plans/graph-storage-lmdb.md:473/485/530, repo-registry-strict-contract.md:4/84, storage-migration-lmdb-lance.md:40/43) or in two test files (src/db/__tests__/entities-lance-integration.test.ts:5-264 and src/db/__tests__/reindex-file-lmdb.test.ts:2-309). The listing stops inside src/db/__tests__/, before any non-test source path is reached; even src/db/entities.ts itself does not appear in the grep output. Paths that sort after that point (src/db/entities.ts, src/indexer/**, src/daemon/** and others) were never listed, so the absence of a non-test call site cannot be confirmed. Nothing shown contradicts the premise: no non-test caller appears in the 50 matches.","action":"Re-run the probe scoped to non-test source so the cap does not hide the result, e.g. `rg -n '\\breindexFile\\s*\\(' src --glob '!**/__tests__/**' --glob '!**/*.test.ts'`, and confirm the only hit is the definition at src/db/entities.ts:363. If any other hit appears, correct the \"0 callers\" claim in s2/lc1 and c15 and revisit whatever the Story builds on reindexFile being unused.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence was cut off at the 50-match cap before any non-test source file was listed, so the zero-caller claim is neither confirmed nor contradicted. No evidence-derived text correction exists; the fix is a narrower re-probe, then a human decision on whether the premise stands as written.","options":["Re-run the grep limited to non-test files under src/ (excluding __tests__ and *.test.ts) and keep the premise unchanged if the only hit is src/db/entities.ts:363.","Re-run the grep with a higher match cap or with docs/ excluded, then re-judge this premise against the full listing.","If the re-probe finds a non-test caller, reword the premise to name the caller(s) and re-check the Story's design for the per-file refresh path.","Accept the premise with a note that it rests on the s1 analyze bundle (c15) and not on this review's truncated probe."]}},{"claimId":"p6","ref":"4. Assumptions / c3","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"reindexFile in src/db/entities.ts replaces a file's entities only; it does not touch relationship (edge) rows or the unresolved-relation queue.","evidence":"UNVERIFIABLE from the gathered evidence, with signs the premise may be wrong on edges. The anchor resolves: `src/db/entities.ts:363` reads `export async function reindexFile(`. But only the signature line was read, so nothing shows what the body does or which helpers it calls. The edge/unresolved write grep shows that `src/db/entities.ts` itself removes edge rows at lines 1150 (`s.outEdge.remove(outKey)`), 1156 (`s.inEdge.remove(inKey)`), 1173 (`s.inEdge.remove(inKey)`) and 1179 (`s.outEdge.remove(outKey)`). The evidence does not identify the enclosing function or whether reindexFile reaches it. In addition, `src/db/__tests__/reindex-file-lmdb.test.ts:215-216` seeds `s.outEdge.put(...)` / `s.inEdge.put(...)` CALLS edges inside the reindexFile test file, which suggests reindexFile has tested edge behaviour. For the unresolved-relation queue, every `unresolved`/`unresolvedByFile` put/remove match is in `src/db/relations.ts` (269, 272, 418, 454, 470, 471) and none is in entities.ts, which is consistent with that half of the premise for direct writes but does not rule out a call from reindexFile into relations.ts.","action":"Read the body of reindexFile (`src/db/entities.ts:363` onward) and the function enclosing `src/db/entities.ts:1150-1179`, and determine whether reindexFile reaches that edge-removal code when it replaces a file's entities. Also read `src/db/__tests__/reindex-file-lmdb.test.ts` around line 215 to see what edge behaviour it asserts. If reindexFile does remove edges of replaced entities, reword assumption c3 and re-check any story that relies on edges surviving a reindex or on a separate edge clean-up step. If it does not, keep c3 and add the body lines as anchors.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the anchor but not the behavioural claim, and the edge removals at src/db/entities.ts:1150-1179 plus the edge seeding in reindex-file-lmdb.test.ts:215-216 leave open that reindexFile does touch edge rows. The correct wording depends on a read of the function body, so no evidence-derived edit can be made yet.","options":["Keep c3 as written, after confirming from the reindexFile body that it never reaches the edge removals at src/db/entities.ts:1150-1179, and add those body lines as anchors.","Reword c3 to say reindexFile replaces a file's entities and removes the edge rows of the entities it deletes (if the body read shows it reaches src/db/entities.ts:1150-1179), while leaving the unresolved-relation queue, written only in src/db/relations.ts, untouched.","Narrow c3 to the part the evidence supports: reindexFile does not write the unresolved-relation queue directly (all unresolved put/remove sites are in src/db/relations.ts), and drop the edge claim until it is verified."]}},{"claimId":"p7","ref":"c4, s1/lc1","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"ISSUE-12f70133's ISSUE.md contains the sentence \"A resolved edge is silently dropped when its target is not stored yet.\" and attributes it to upsertResolvedRelations skipping an edge whose endpoint is missing.","evidence":"The quoted sentence is not confirmed by the gathered evidence. The read of docs/standalone/bug-code-graph-missing-most-cross-E2026100712f70133/ISSUE.md:21 returned \"Three faults that compound, plus one separate gap.\" — a summary heading line, not the sentence \"A resolved edge is silently dropped when its target is not stored yet.\" No probe grepped ISSUE.md for that sentence, so whether it appears elsewhere in the file, and whether it is attributed to upsertResolvedRelations, is not shown. The function itself does exist: the read of src/db/relations.ts:120 returned \"async function upsertResolvedRelations(relations: Relation[]): Promise<void> {\". The grep for /export async function upsertResolvedRelations/ returned 0 matches, which is consistent with that line: the function is declared without `export`. The /skipped/ grep was truncated at 50 matches, all in unrelated files (camon/, design/analyze-framework.md, docs/daemon.md, docs/epics/add-bugfix-triage-category-.../S003–S005); none is in src/db/relations.ts or the cited ISSUE.md, so the skip-on-missing-endpoint behaviour is neither confirmed nor refuted. Net: the symbol anchor is right, the ISSUE.md line anchor points at a different sentence, and the attribution is unverified.","action":"Re-anchor the citation. Grep the cited ISSUE.md for \"silently dropped\" to find the line that actually carries the sentence and replace `:21` with it, or drop the line number if the sentence is a paraphrase rather than a verbatim quote. Separately confirm the skip-on-missing-endpoint behaviour by reading the body of upsertResolvedRelations from src/db/relations.ts:120 onward. Do not describe the function as exported: it is module-private at line 120.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence shows ISSUE.md:21 holds a different sentence, but it does not show where (or whether) the quoted sentence appears, so the correct line number cannot be derived from the evidence. No artifact edit is safe without one more probe.","options":["Grep the cited ISSUE.md for \"silently dropped\" and re-anchor the premise to the line that actually holds the sentence.","If the sentence is not verbatim in ISSUE.md, reword the premise as a paraphrase of the fault list introduced at ISSUE.md:21 and remove the quotation marks.","Drop the cross-artifact quote and ground the claim directly in the code by citing the skip branch inside upsertResolvedRelations (src/db/relations.ts:120 onward) after reading it."]}},{"claimId":"p8","ref":"s1/ac2","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"ISSUE-12f70133's reproduction expects 4 callers of runGrepSearch and 1 caller of pickScope, the figures s1/ac2 uses as its acceptance measure.","evidence":"The reads of the cited anchors do not show the figures. ISSUE.md:9 reads \"On a repo that has had one full index (this repo, indexed 2026-07-14, status ready, no pending jobs):\", ISSUE.md:10 is blank, and ISSUE.md:35 is \"## Fix intent\". None of the lines handed to me states \"4 callers of runGrepSearch\" or \"1 caller of pickScope\", so the claim that the ISSUE's reproduction expects those figures is unverifiable from this evidence.\n\nThe source grep partly corroborates the first figure independently: /\\brunGrepSearch\\(/ has exactly 4 non-test, non-definition call sites in src — src/analyze/explore/config-trace.ts:104, src/analyze/explore/search-text.ts:104, src/daemon/tools/builtins/search/grep.ts:217 and src/workflow/review/probe.ts:62 (grep.ts:102 is the definition; the rest are __tests__ or docs).\n\nThe second figure is not corroborated: /\\bpickScope\\(/ returned 9 matches, all under docs/epics/.../measurements, with no match in src at all — neither a definition nor a call site. So the \"1 caller of pickScope\" figure is neither confirmed nor contradicted by the gathered evidence.","action":"Re-anchor the premise to the ISSUE.md lines that actually state the expected caller counts (the reproduction block lies somewhere between line 11 and line 34, which were not read) and confirm they say 4 for runGrepSearch and 1 for pickScope. Separately confirm the pickScope figure against source, since the grep found no `pickScope(` text in src; if the single caller invokes it in a form the pattern misses, say so in s1/ac2 so the acceptance measure is reproducible.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchors point at the reproduction's preamble rather than at the lines carrying the figures, and the pickScope count has no supporting source match in the evidence. The prescribed acceptance measure may still be right, so this is an anchor and verification gap, not a demonstrated defect. No artifact edit is safe, because the correct line numbers and the pickScope count are not in the evidence.","options":["Re-anchor s1/ac2 to the exact ISSUE.md lines in the reproduction block (between lines 11 and 34) that state the expected 4 and 1 caller counts, after reading them.","Keep the runGrepSearch figure of 4 (corroborated by the four src call sites: config-trace.ts:104, search-text.ts:104, grep.ts:217, probe.ts:62) and re-verify the pickScope figure of 1 against source before using it as an acceptance measure.","Restate s1/ac2 to name the expected caller files explicitly rather than bare counts, so the measure does not depend on the ISSUE's wording or on how the callers are counted (tests and the definition excluded)."]}},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"S001:FR003, s1/ac3","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"ISSUE-12f70133 records that a re-export line produces no relation, so a call that reaches a function through a re-exporting file (pickScope via src/analyze/classifier/index.ts) is never linked.","evidence":"The evidence does not confirm the premise. The read of docs/standalone/bug-code-graph-missing-most-cross-E2026100712f70133/ISSUE.md:29 returned a paragraph about a different cause: stale first-pass indexing (\"of the 23 files in `src/analyze/explore`, 21 import the logger; the graph holds exactly 4 logger edges...\", the `runGrepSearch` caller in `probe.ts`, and `search-text.ts` resolving all three imports). That line says nothing about re-export lines producing no relation, and does not mention `pickScope` or `src/analyze/classifier/index.ts`. The grep for /export \\{[^}]*pickScope[^}]*\\} from/ returned 0 matches, so no named re-export of `pickScope` was found anywhere, including in the anchored src/analyze/classifier/index.ts. The 3 `export_statement` matches (src/indexer/parser/typescript.ts:65, :69, :189) only show the parser has an `export_statement` case; they do not show whether a re-export emits a relation. No evidence was gathered on whether `pickScope` is re-exported in another form (for example `export * from`), so the premise is unverifiable rather than contradicted.","action":"Re-anchor the premise to the ISSUE.md line that actually records the re-export cause; line 29 is the stale-index evidence paragraph. Then confirm how, or whether, src/analyze/classifier/index.ts re-exports `pickScope` (named `export { … } from`, `export * from`, or import-then-export), since the named-form grep found nothing. If `pickScope` is not re-exported through that file, replace the example in FR003 / s1 ac3 with a real re-exported symbol so the acceptance check exercises an existing re-export path.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited ISSUE.md:29 covers a different cause (stale first-pass index) and the named re-export grep for pickScope found 0 matches, so both the anchor and the concrete example are unconfirmed. The evidence supplies no correct line number or re-export form, so no mechanical edit can be derived; a human must pick the corrected anchor and example.","options":["Re-anchor the premise to the ISSUE.md line that actually states re-export lines produce no relation, and keep the pickScope example after confirming classifier/index.ts re-exports it via `export * from` or import-then-export.","Keep the re-export claim but replace the pickScope / src/analyze/classifier/index.ts example with a symbol verified to be re-exported with a named `export { … } from` line, and update s1 ac3 to match.","If ISSUE-12f70133 does not record a re-export cause at all, drop the cross-artifact attribution and restate FR003 as a new finding of this artifact, grounded in the `export_statement` handling at src/indexer/parser/typescript.ts:189."]}},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"FR002","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"A reindex today skips files whose content is unchanged, so relationships lost on the first pass are never rebuilt without removing and re-adding the repository.","evidence":"UNVERIFIABLE from the gathered evidence. Both greps (/hash\\s*===|contentHash|unchanged/ and /skip/) were truncated at the 50-match cap, and no returned match is in the anchored file src/indexer/index.ts or anywhere under src/indexer/. The matches are all in unrelated places: camon/ bootstrap scripts, docs/daemon.md, design/analyze-*.md, and the bugfix-triage epic's DEF/HLD/LLD/PLAN files. The only match that touches the topic is design/indexer.html:778 (\"recompute hash — skip re-indexing if unchanged. Handles editor save-without-change events.\"). That is a design document, not the code, and it describes the save-without-change path for file events, not what a full reindex does. The one read, docs/standalone/bug-code-graph-missing-most-cross-E2026100712f70133/ISSUE.md:21, returned \"Three faults that compound, plus one separate gap.\" — the artifact's own prose, which says nothing about skip behaviour. Nothing in the evidence shows that a reindex in src/indexer/index.ts skips files with unchanged content, or that removing and re-adding the repository is the only way to rebuild lost relationships.","action":"Re-probe the anchored file directly before relying on FR002: scope the grep to src/indexer/ (e.g. /hash|unchanged|skip/ in src/indexer/index.ts and the reindex entry point it calls) and read the lines where the stored file hash is compared and the file is skipped. Then cite that `path:line` in FR002. If the skip only applies to the file-event path (as design/indexer.html:778 describes) and a full reindex re-parses every file, reword FR002 and re-check the fix intent that depends on it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise may well be true, but the evidence handed over does not reach the anchored code: the greps ran over the whole tree, hit the cap on documentation matches, and returned nothing from src/indexer/. No text edit can be derived from this evidence, so the choice is between re-probing with a narrower scope and rewording the premise.","options":["Re-run the probe scoped to src/indexer/ and add the exact `path:line` of the unchanged-content skip to FR002, keeping the premise text as is if the code confirms it.","If the scoped probe shows the skip exists only on the file-event path (design/indexer.html:778) and not on a full reindex, reword FR002 to say which path skips unchanged files, and revisit the fix intent that depends on it.","If the scoped probe shows a reindex does skip unchanged files but another route (e.g. a forced or full reindex option) rebuilds relationships, drop the claim that removing and re-adding the repository is the only way, and keep the rest."]}},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"c5, s3/lc1","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"ISSUE-61045de9's ISSUE.md states \"The graph store keeps entity rows keyed by a numeric id and has no index by repository or by file.\" and that findEntitiesByFile and listEntitiesForRepo in src/db/entities.ts iterate the whole entity table and filter row by row.","evidence":"Partly confirmed, partly unverifiable. Confirmed: both functions exist in the named file — `src/db/entities.ts:779: export async function listEntitiesForRepo(_db: DbClient, repo: string): Promise<Entity[]>` and `src/db/entities.ts:824: export async function findEntitiesByFile(_db: DbClient, file: string): Promise<Entity[]>`. Not confirmed: (1) the read of ISSUE.md:11 returned only the heading `## Root cause`, not the quoted sentence \"The graph store keeps entity rows keyed by a numeric id and has no index by repository or by file.\", so the verbatim quote is not in the evidence (the anchor points at the section heading, the right concept, but not the sentence). (2) The `getRange(` grep was truncated at its 50-match cap; the only src/db/entities.ts hits shown are :417 (`for (const { key, value } of s.entity.getRange()) {`) and :655 (`store.repo.getRange()`), both outside the two functions' bodies at :779 and :824. No line inside either function was read, so the claim that they iterate the whole entity table and filter row by row is not shown. Indirect support only: ISSUE.md:21 cites c1 `src/db/entities.ts` — \"for (const { key, value } of store.entity.getRange()) {\", and an unrelated LLD (add-new-repo-stats…/S001/LLD.md:266) states \"no per-repo entity index exists\" — neither is a read of the two function bodies.","action":"Re-probe before relying on this premise: read ISSUE.md lines 12–14 to confirm the quoted root-cause sentence verbatim, and read src/db/entities.ts around :779–:822 and :824–:860 to confirm each function does an unbounded `store.entity.getRange()` scan with a per-row filter. If both hold, re-anchor the quote to the actual line of the sentence (line 11 is the `## Root cause` heading) and cite the two function bodies by line. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise, so the prescribed change is not shown to be wrong.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The functions and file are real and the anchor lands on the right section, so this is an unverified-detail problem rather than a contradiction. No safe artifact edit can be derived: the evidence shows neither the line that carries the quoted sentence nor the lines of the full-table scans inside the two functions, so any replacement line number would be invented.","options":["Re-run the probes with a read of ISSUE.md:12-14 and of src/db/entities.ts:779-860, then re-anchor the quote and the scan claim to the lines actually found.","Keep the premise but re-anchor the quote to the section rather than a line (ISSUE.md '## Root cause'), and cite the functions at src/db/entities.ts:779 and :824 as the scan sites pending a body read.","Soften the premise to what the evidence supports: the two functions exist at src/db/entities.ts:779 and :824, and ISSUE.md's root-cause section cites a `store.entity.getRange()` loop in that file (ISSUE.md:21, c1)."]}},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"S003:FR002, s3/ac4","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The scope check ensureNonEmptyClosure in src/analyze/context/invariants.ts asks for every entity of a repository (listEntitiesForRepo) only to learn whether the repository holds any stored entity.","evidence":"The evidence confirms the premise. The whole-repository read sits inside the check: src/analyze/context/invariants.ts:235-236 reads `? await deps.listEntitiesForRepo(best.path) : await listEntitiesForRepo(null, best.path);`. The function body captured in S008/measurements/live/code-S.run/tasks/t06.json:473 and the signature in S007/LLD.md:56 show `ensureNonEmptyClosure(scope: ResolvedScope, deps?: Pick<ScopeDeps, 'listRepos' | 'listEntitiesForRepo'>): Promise<string | undefined>`. The result is a repo path or undefined, so the entity list never leaves the function. The test titles show the list is used only as an empty / non-empty test: 'registered repo with entities -> returns repo path' and 'registered repo with ZERO entities -> ScopeNotIndexedError' (S007/baseline/analyze.txt:272, :274). S006/LLD.md:140 describes the same test: 'a registered repo with no stored entities fails as ScopeNotIndexedError'. src/db/entities.ts (HLD.md:404) declares `listEntitiesForRepo(_db, repo): Promise<Entity[]>`, so the call does return every entity of the repository. The lines after invariants.ts:236 that consume the result were not in the evidence; the 'only' rests on the return type and the test titles.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"c10, k5","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"src/db/graph/store.ts declares `export const SCHEMA_VERSION = 4;`, and the store applies schema migrations when the daemon opens it.","evidence":"The citation half is confirmed: the read of src/db/graph/store.ts:128 returns `export const SCHEMA_VERSION = 4;`, and the grep for `export const SCHEMA_VERSION = \\d+` matches the same line (the DEF's own c10 citation at DEF.md:358 quotes it verbatim). The second half — that the store applies schema migrations when the daemon opens it — is not addressed by the gathered evidence. The `/migrat/` grep was truncated at its 50-match cap and every match shown is in AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, design/ or docs/ (e.g. CLAUDE.md:149 `docs/plans/storage-migration-lmdb-lance.md`, docs/daemon.md:88/111/417/420 about config and Ollama migration); none is in src/db/graph/store.ts or any other source file. The `/SCHEMA_VERSION/` grep was also truncated at 50 and shows only docs matches, so no use site of the constant in an open-time migration path was gathered. The migrate-on-open claim is therefore unverifiable from this evidence, neither confirmed nor contradicted.","action":"Keep the `SCHEMA_VERSION = 4` citation as is. Before anything relies on the migrate-on-open behaviour, re-probe it with a search scoped to src/db/graph/ (e.g. uses of SCHEMA_VERSION and the migration/upgrade routine in store.ts's open path) and cite the specific file:line where the stored version is compared and migrations run. If no such code is found, reword the premise to state only the version constant.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited constant is verified at src/db/graph/store.ts:128, so the anchor is sound. The behavioural claim about migrations on open has no source evidence in the gathered probes (both broad greps hit their cap on docs matches before reaching src/), so it needs either a scoped re-probe with a concrete anchor or a narrower wording. No artifact edit is proposed because the evidence does not supply a replacement value or line.","options":["Re-run the probe scoped to src/db/graph/ and add a file:line anchor for the open-time migration code alongside store.ts:128, leaving the premise text unchanged.","Narrow the premise to the verified fact only: store.ts declares `export const SCHEMA_VERSION = 4;`, dropping the migrate-on-open clause.","Keep the premise as written and record the migrate-on-open clause as an unverified assumption to be confirmed during design of the Story that bumps the schema version."]}},{"claimId":"p14","ref":"k6","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Entity and relation kinds are stored as fixed byte values through an explicit kind-to-byte table in the graph layer, so adding a kind does not require renumbering existing ones.","evidence":"The evidence confirms an explicit kind-to-byte table in the graph layer, in src/db/graph/keys.ts. Two maps exist there: RELATION_KIND_BYTE and ENTITY_KIND_BYTE, with the reverse lookup `kindByteToName(b: number): RelationKind` at src/db/graph/keys.ts:53. Earlier review rows quoted in the evidence (S001/LLD.md:224-226) place `export type RelationKind = keyof typeof RELATION_KIND_BYTE` at keys.ts:37 and `export type EntityKind = keyof typeof ENTITY_KIND_BYTE` at keys.ts:77.\n\nCallers look the byte up by kind name rather than by position: src/db/entities.ts:291 (`encodeNameIndexKey(repoId, ENTITY_KIND_BYTE[row.kind], row.name)`), src/db/entities.ts:674 and :710, src/db/graph/migrations.ts:125, src/db/graph/__tests__/traversal.test.ts:45 (`RELATION_KIND_BYTE[kind]`), and src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:215-218 (`b === RELATION_KIND_BYTE.CALLS`). The byte is a single u8 in the key: keys.ts:146/157/177 guard `kind < 0 || kind > 0xff`, and keys.ts:250 writes `Buffer.from([kindByte & 0xff])`.\n\nAdding a kind without renumbering is shown by precedent: S001/PLAN.md:21 appended CALLS_HTTP=13, PUBLISHES_TO=14, SUBSCRIBES_TO=15, CALLS_RPC=16 and 'externalEndpoint'=13 \"(append-only, no reorder)\", and PLAN.md:24 requires \"no reordered or reused existing byte\". src/db/graph/__tests__/sc1-external-endpoint.test.ts:84-86 and store.test.ts:184 check the round trip through kindByteToName.\n\nTwo limits on this evidence: the table literal itself (keys.ts around lines 20-80) was not read, so the fixed values are known from the plan text and tests rather than from the source lines; and no match places a kind-byte table in the anchored src/db/graph/codec.ts — every hit is in keys.ts. Neither changes what gets built.","action":"none — verified sound. Optional cleanup: the kind-to-byte tables are in src/db/graph/keys.ts only, so the src/db/graph/codec.ts anchor could be dropped from this premise.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"c16, s6/lc1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/indexer/index.ts applies a list of bare names to every segment of every path: `return relative(repoPath, file).split(sep).some(seg => ignoreSet.has(seg));`.","evidence":"The read of src/indexer/index.ts:104 returns exactly `return relative(repoPath, file).split(sep).some(seg => ignoreSet.has(seg));`, matching the quoted citation verbatim. The grep for `ignoreSet` shows line 103 declares `function hasIgnoredSegment(file: string, repoPath: string, ignoreSet: Set<string>): boolean`, so the set holds bare string names and the check tests every path segment of the repo-relative path against it. The helper is applied to every listed file at src/indexer/index.ts:124 (`.filter(f => !hasIgnoredSegment(f, repoPath, ignoreSet))`) and again at src/indexer/index.ts:502 (`const ignored = hasIgnoredSegment(file, repoPath, ignoreSet);`). The legacy walker at src/indexer/index.ts:140 (`if (ignoreSet.has(entry.name)) continue;`) applies the same bare-name match per directory entry. The anchor file:line and the `ignoreSet` symbol both resolve to the cited concept.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p16","ref":"c17","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"A full walk in src/indexer/index.ts follows the repository's own ignore files by listing files with git: `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z'],`.","evidence":"The read of src/indexer/index.ts:120 returned FOUND with the exact line `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z'],`, matching the premise's quoted text verbatim at the cited anchor. The grep confirms the same match at src/indexer/index.ts:120 (plus an identical flag set at src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113, whose comment in the t06.json capture says \"Same flags the indexer uses\", and the DEF.md:365 citation entry itself). The `--exclude-standard` flag is what makes git honour the repository's own ignore files, consistent with the premise's claim. The evidence does not show the surrounding lines, so the \"full walk\" framing of the enclosing function is not directly shown, but the cited line and its ignore-file semantics are confirmed.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"c18","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/indexer/repo-ignore-config.ts returns an already-stored ignore list unchanged (`return existing.ignore;   // respect a user-authored / prior config`), so later edits to the repository's ignore files never reach the list.","evidence":"The read of src/indexer/repo-ignore-config.ts:87 returns `return existing.ignore;   // respect a user-authored / prior config`, verbatim as the premise quotes it. The grep for /existing\\.ignore/ also shows the guard at src/indexer/repo-ignore-config.ts:86: `if (existing?.ignore !== undefined && existing.ignore.length > 0) {`. So a stored, non-empty ignore list is returned unchanged, which supports the premise's consequence that later edits to the repository's ignore files do not reach an already-stored list. The anchor resolves to the cited file and line. The only nuance is that the early return applies when the stored list is non-empty (line 86), which does not change the premise's conclusion.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p18","ref":"1. Problem / c16-c18","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"A single file event (watcher / single-file index path) is checked against the bare-name ignore list alone and never against the repository's ignore files; the git ls-files exclusion is used only by the full walk.","evidence":"Partly confirmed, partly unverifiable from the gathered evidence.\n\nConfirmed:\n- `src/indexer/index.ts:104` reads `return relative(repoPath, file).split(sep).some(seg => ignoreSet.has(seg));` — a per-path check against the bare-name ignore set only.\n- `src/indexer/index.ts:120` reads `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z'],` and is the only `--exclude-standard` call under `src/indexer/`; `src/indexer/index.ts:140` (`if (ignoreSet.has(entry.name)) continue;`) is the only other `ignoreSet.has(` site.\n- `check-ignore` has 0 matches, and `isIgnored(` / `shouldIgnore(` have 0 matches, so no per-file git ignore query exists under those names.\n\nNot shown:\n- `src/daemon/watcher.ts` is an anchor but was neither read nor matched by any probe. The evidence does not show that the watcher / single-file path actually calls the line-104 check, nor rule out an ignore-file filter there under another name (e.g. a watcher `ignored` option or a .gitignore-parsing library).\n\nImprecise:\n- \"the git ls-files exclusion is used only by the full walk\" holds within the indexer, but `src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113` also runs `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z']`, so it is not the only user in the repo.\n\nNeither gap contradicts the premise or changes the prescribed change (make the single-file path honour the repository's ignore files).","action":"Read `src/daemon/watcher.ts` and the single-file index entry point to confirm that the only filter on that path is the line-104 bare-name check, then cite the specific `watcher.ts` line(s) in the premise. Scope the \"only by the full walk\" statement to the indexer, noting that `src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113` runs the same listing for the analyze side.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The indexer-side facts are confirmed at src/indexer/index.ts:104 and :120, and no check-ignore call exists anywhere. The watcher half of the premise rests on an anchor with no line number and no gathered read, and the \"only by the full walk\" wording overlooks the analyze-side filter at src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113. Both are corrections of wording and anchoring that need a human to confirm the watcher call site; the prescribed change is unaffected. No artifact edit is proposed because the artifact's exact text was not part of the evidence.","options":["Read src/daemon/watcher.ts, add the exact line where the single-file path applies the bare-name check (or calls the index.ts:104 helper), and keep the premise as written.","Reword the premise to scope it to the indexer: \"within src/indexer, the git ls-files exclusion is used only by the full walk (index.ts:120); the analyze-side filter at src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113 runs the same listing separately\".","Do both: pin the watcher.ts line anchor and scope the ls-files statement to the indexer."]}},{"claimId":"p19","ref":"S006:FR007, s6/ac9","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"A folder that is not under version control is walked by a non-git fallback in src/indexer/index.ts that does not read .gitignore files, so today it is filtered by the bare-name list only.","evidence":"Partly supported, but the key negative claim is unverifiable from the gathered evidence. Supported: src/indexer/index.ts:120 reads `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z']` (the git path honours .gitignore), and src/indexer/index.ts:128 logs 'git ls-files failed, falling back to directory walker', so a non-git fallback walker does exist in that file. src/indexer/index.ts:100 ('ignore set. Applies the ignore list to `git ls-files` output too') implies an ignore set is also applied to the walker. Not shown: the fallback walker's body. The `.gitignore` grep was TRUNCATED at the 50-match cap and every returned match is under docs/ or jetbrains-plugin/, so it gives no information on whether src/indexer/index.ts mentions or reads .gitignore. The `readdir(|walkDir|fallback` grep was also truncated at 50 with only docs/design matches, so the walker itself was never read. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise; nothing confirms that the walker 'does not read .gitignore files' or that it is filtered 'by the bare-name list only'.","action":"Re-run the probes scoped to src/indexer/index.ts (grep `.gitignore`, `readdir`, and the walker function name in that file only, and read the walker body following line 128) to confirm that the fallback reads no .gitignore and filters only by the bare-name ignore set. Then anchor the premise to the walker's actual `path:line` rather than the whole file.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the fallback exists (index.ts:128) but both relevant greps hit the match cap before reaching src/indexer, so the claim that the walker ignores .gitignore cannot be confirmed or corrected mechanically. No evidence-derived text edit is available; a scoped re-probe is needed first.","options":["Re-probe scoped to src/indexer/index.ts and, if the walker body shows no .gitignore read, keep the premise and add the walker's exact path:line anchor.","If the scoped re-probe shows the walker does read .gitignore (or applies more than the bare-name list), reword the premise and re-check S006:FR007 / s6/ac9, which depend on it.","Keep the premise as written and record it as accepted-unverified with a note that the build task must read the walker body before changing it."]}},{"claimId":"p20","ref":"FR012, s9/ac4","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"The index reads exactly six programming languages today (the Language union in src/shared/types.ts and the registered tree-sitter parsers), and none of Rust, C#, SQL, C or C++ is among them.","evidence":"The premise is only partly supported, and two of its facts are contradicted by the gathered evidence.\n\n1. Anchor resolves correctly: `src/shared/types.ts:518` reads `export type Language = 'python' | 'go' | 'typescript' | 'javascript'` — the right entity, but the read shows only the first line of a multi-line union (lines 519-520 were not gathered).\n\n2. 'sql' IS already a member of the union: the grep hit `src/shared/types.ts:521:  | 'sql' | 'proto' | 'graphql' | 'dockerfile' | 'config';` is the closing line of that same `Language` declaration. `src/indexer/parser/artifact.ts:40` (`'.sql': 'sql'`) shows `.sql` files are already mapped to it, as an artifact language rather than a tree-sitter-parsed one. So \"none of ... SQL ... is among them\" is false for the Language union.\n\n3. The count \"exactly six\" looks stale: `package-lock.json:63-69` lists seven tree-sitter grammars (go, java, javascript, kotlin, python, scala, typescript), which correspond to seven programming languages (python, go, typescript, javascript, java, scala, kotlin). The Kotlin story's CR.md:27 cites `src/indexer/parser/kotlin.ts:595`, indicating the Kotlin parser was built. The evidence did not include the full union or the parser registry, so the exact count of six vs seven is not directly confirmed.\n\n4. Rust, C#, C and C++ absence is supported: no `'rust'`, `'csharp'`, `'c_sharp'` or `'cpp'` hit lies in the Language union or under `src/indexer/parser`. The only hits are `src/indexer/repo-type.ts:16` (`RepoType`, a different type) and `docs/plans/repo-registry-strict-contract.md:159` (\"rust ... future\").\n\nMateriality: the prescribed direction (these languages lack real symbol-level parsing today) still holds for Rust, C#, C and C++, so this is not HIGH on the evidence given. The SQL part is the real risk: a task that treats 'sql' as a brand-new Language member and `.sql` as an unclaimed extension would collide with the existing artifact mapping. Whether that breaks the build cannot be stated from this evidence, because the registry and the artifact's SQL task text were not gathered.","action":"Correct the premise in the artifact: (a) restate the count from the full `Language` union at `src/shared/types.ts:518-521` and the registered parsers in `src/indexer/parser` (the evidence points to seven programming languages including Kotlin, not six); (b) drop SQL from the \"not among them\" list and state that 'sql' already exists in the union as an artifact language (`src/shared/types.ts:521`, `src/indexer/parser/artifact.ts:40`) without symbol-level parsing; (c) make the SQL story/acceptance check say explicitly how the new SQL parser coexists with or replaces the existing `.sql` artifact mapping. Before approval, read `src/shared/types.ts:518-521` in full and the parser registry to confirm the exact count.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The count and the SQL claim are both contradicted or unconfirmed by the evidence, but fixing the SQL part needs a decision on how the new parser relates to the existing `.sql` artifact mapping, so a human should pick the wording. No artifactEdits are given because the artifact's verbatim text was not supplied and the exact language count was not fully gathered (lines 519-520 of the union are missing).","options":["Reword to: \"The index parses seven programming languages with tree-sitter today (python, go, typescript, javascript, java, scala, kotlin); none of Rust, C#, C or C++ is among them, and 'sql' exists in the Language union only as an artifact language (src/shared/types.ts:521, src/indexer/parser/artifact.ts:40) with no symbol-level parsing.\" — after confirming the count against the full union.","Keep the premise scoped to tree-sitter parsers only (drop the Language-union claim), fix the count, and add a separate premise covering the existing 'sql' artifact mapping and how the SQL story upgrades it.","Re-scope the SQL story: treat it as upgrading an existing Language member ('sql') from artifact handling to symbol-level parsing, and amend s9/ac4 so it no longer asserts SQL is absent from the union."]}},{"claimId":"p21","ref":"1. Problem, c15","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"The index holds no grouping of related code into subsystems: there is no community-detection or clustering code (Louvain / Leiden / community / subsystem) anywhere under src/.","evidence":"All three probes re-run against the source tree returned nothing: grep /(?i)louvain|leiden/ → 0 matches; grep /(?i)community.?detect|detectCommunit/ → 0 matches; grep /(?i)subsystem/ → 0 matches. This confirms the premise that no Louvain/Leiden, community-detection or subsystem-grouping code exists under src/. Caveat: the probes did not search for a bare \"cluster\" or bare \"community\" term, so the \"clustering\" part of the claim is confirmed only through the named algorithms and the subsystem term, not by a direct search.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p22","ref":"1. Problem","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"The index records no relationships between documents: no relation kind or indexer step links one document to another or a document to the code it describes.","evidence":"Partly supported, not fully verifiable. The anchor resolves: `export type RelationKind =` is at src/shared/types.ts:539 (mirrored at src/db/graph/keys.ts:43 as `keyof typeof RELATION_KIND_BYTE`). The grep for candidate document-link kinds ('LINKS_TO', 'DOCUMENTS', 'DESCRIBES', 'MENTIONS') returned no match anywhere in src/; the only hit of that group is 'REFERENCES' (src/shared/types.ts:547), which docs/plans/graph-storage-lmdb.md:352 describes as `function/class → type/variable (general use)` and which src/daemon/tools/builtins/code/class-references.ts:41-43 uses as a code-to-code reference kind — not a document relation. That is consistent with the premise. Two gaps remain: (1) the body of the union was never read, and it is wider than the eight kinds listed at src/daemon/index.ts:1117 — src/daemon/tools/builtins/graph/index.ts:231 also lists 'CONTAINS' and 'STEP_DEPENDS_ON' — so a document-linking kind under a name the grep did not try cannot be ruled out; (2) no evidence was gathered on the second half of the claim, that no indexer step links a document to another document or to code.","action":"Close the two gaps before relying on the premise: read the full union at src/shared/types.ts:539 onward (and RELATION_KIND_BYTE in src/db/graph/keys.ts) to enumerate every member, checking what 'CONTAINS' connects in particular; and grep src/indexer/ for any step that emits relations from document entities. If neither turns up a document relation, the premise stands as written and no artifact edit is needed.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence contradicts nothing in the premise, so there is no evidence-derived text correction to make. What is missing is a complete enumeration of the union and a probe of the indexer, and the outcome of those decides whether the wording needs to change.","options":["Re-probe: read the full RelationKind union and RELATION_KIND_BYTE, and grep src/indexer/ for relation emission from document entities; keep the premise unchanged if nothing is found.","Narrow the premise to what the evidence supports: no relation kind named for document linking exists in RelationKind, and 'REFERENCES' is a code-to-code kind. Drop the indexer-step claim until it is probed.","If the re-probe shows 'CONTAINS' or another kind already connects documents (for example document to section), reword the premise to say that no kind links one document to another or a document to the code it describes, and name the existing kind as out of scope."]}},{"claimId":"p23","ref":"3. Non-goals (embedding keep-alive)","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every embedding call in the indexer already asks for the model to stay loaded: each `ollama.embed(` call in src/indexer/embedder.ts passes `keep_alive: EMBEDDING_KEEP_ALIVE`.","evidence":"The grep for `.embed(` finds exactly three `ollama.embed(` call sites in src/indexer/embedder.ts: line 156, line 215 and line 247 (line 35 is a comment, not a call). Each carries the keep-alive: line 156 reads `ollama.embed({ model: EMBEDDING_MODEL, input: inputs, keep_alive: EMBEDDING_KEEP_ALIVE })`; line 218 reads `keep_alive: EMBEDDING_KEEP_ALIVE,`, three lines into the multi-line call opened at 215; line 250 reads `keep_alive: EMBEDDING_KEEP_ALIVE,`, three lines into the multi-line call opened at 247. All three anchors resolve to the cited concept. The set is also guarded by src/indexer/__tests__/embedder-keepalive.test.ts:27 ('every ollama.embed(...) call in the indexer embedder passes keep_alive'), and line 24 of that test asserts the value is sourced from `_localDefaults.embeddingKeepAlive`. No `ollama.embed(` call in the file lacks a matching keep_alive read.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: all three `ollama.embed(` calls in src/indexer/embedder.ts pass `keep_alive: EMBEDDING_KEEP_ALIVE`, and the anchors point at the right lines. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p24","ref":"c19, s10/lc2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"docs/daemon.md configures the mid tier as `{ \"runner\": \"cli-claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet\" }`, so the mid-tier model is reached through a CLI provider on the user's own session.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve to the quoted text. `read docs/daemon.md:381` → `\"mid\":   { \"runner\": \"cli-claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet\" },` and `read docs/daemon.md:454` → `\"mid\":   { \"runner\": \"cli-claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet\" },`. The tier table at docs/daemon.md:438 agrees: `| mid | cli-claude / sonnet | synthesis, grounding/context assembly, HLD framework/rollout |`. That `cli-claude` is a CLI provider on the user's own session is supported by docs/daemon.md:70 (`Claude Code / Codex session (tiers.core = { runner: cli-claude / cli-codex } ...)` — \"big shaper LLM lives in the CLI\") and docs/daemon.md:486 (`Every resolved runner is one of ollama | cli-claude | cli-codex`). The artifact's own citation c19 at DEF.md:367 quotes the same string verbatim.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p25","ref":"c6-c9, k1-k4","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"CLAUDE.md contains verbatim the four rules cited as constraints: \"Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority.\", \"Daemon owns all DB access\", \"No direct cloud REST calls from our process.\" and \"always serial `for...of` with sequential awaits.\"","evidence":"Three of the four quoted rules are confirmed verbatim by the gathered evidence; the fourth is not addressed by any probe.\n\n- Confirmed: read CLAUDE.md:11 → \"- **Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority.** When choosing between an accurate-but-expensive path ...\"\n- Confirmed: read CLAUDE.md:12 → \"- **No direct cloud REST calls from our process.** Cloud LLM access happens through the locally-installed `claude` and `codex` CLI binaries ...\"\n- Confirmed: grep /Promise\\.all/ → CLAUDE.md:122: \"- Never `Promise.all` anything that reaches an LLM provider (cloud or local Ollama embed / complete); always serial `for...of` with sequential awaits.\"\n- Not addressed: \"Daemon owns all DB access\". The only other CLAUDE.md reads returned are line 99 (\"## Code conventions\") and line 109 (\"- `strict: true` with `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`\"), neither of which contains the phrase. The grep was for /Promise\\.all/ only, and its one hit on the phrase is second-hand, in another artifact (docs/epics/add-bugfix-triage-category-insrc-framework-E202609181703991c/S003/LLD.md:230: \"daemon owns all DB access (CLAUDE.md rules 1/4)\"), not in CLAUDE.md itself.\n\nNothing in the evidence contradicts the premise; one of its four verbatim claims is simply unverified by the probes that ran.","action":"Re-run the premise with a probe that targets the missing quote: grep CLAUDE.md for /Daemon owns all DB access/ (expected under \"## Key architectural rules\", rule 1) and read the matching line. If it matches verbatim, downgrade this finding to LOW with no artifact change. If the wording differs, correct the quoted text in the artifact's constraint citation to match the real line.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Three of four quotes are verified verbatim at CLAUDE.md:11, :12 and :122. The fourth (\"Daemon owns all DB access\") was not covered by any gathered read or grep, so no evidence-derived text edit can be proposed; the fix is to complete the verification rather than change the artifact. The prescribed constraints do not change either way, so this is non-blocking.","options":["Add a grep probe for /Daemon owns all DB access/ on CLAUDE.md, re-run the review of this premise, and resolve as verified if it matches verbatim.","Accept with a note: three of four quotes verified by probe (CLAUDE.md:11, :12, :122); the fourth was not probed and is accepted on the reviewer's own check of CLAUDE.md's \"Key architectural rules\" section.","If the re-probe shows different wording, replace the quoted rule in the artifact's citation with the exact CLAUDE.md line text and its line number."]}},{"claimId":"p26","ref":"c12, s4/lc1","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"docs/standalone/defect-installed-daemon-runs-out-lmdb-E202610100d577569/ISSUE.md exists, is already approved, and states that the installed daemon runs out of LMDB locks (reader slots) and then cannot be stopped.","evidence":"Only the existence part of the premise is confirmed: `read docs/standalone/defect-installed-daemon-runs-out-lmdb-E202610100d577569/ISSUE.md:1 → FOUND: <!-- insrc:artifact ISSUE-0d5775698fd042a7 -->`, so the file exists and is an insrc ISSUE artifact. Nothing in the evidence shows it is approved: no `approvedAt` or approval stamp line was read. Nothing shows what it states: `grep /maxReaders|MDB_READERS_FULL|READERS_FULL/ → 0 match(es)`, and the `grep /SIGTERM|shutdown/` result (50 matches, truncated at cap) lists only design/indexer.html, docs/daemon.md and the JetBrains-settings epic files (DEF.md, HLD.md, S002/LLD.md, S002/PLAN.md, S002/CR.md) — no line from the ISSUE.md. The \"runs out of LMDB locks (reader slots) and then cannot be stopped\" content and the approved status are therefore unverifiable from this evidence; the directory slug `defect-installed-daemon-runs-out-lmdb` is consistent with the topic but is not proof of the text.","action":"Re-probe the ISSUE.md directly before relying on this premise: read its header/meta for the approval stamp (`approvedAt`) and read the problem statement lines to confirm it describes LMDB lock/reader-slot exhaustion and the daemon then failing to stop. Use probes that match the issue's own wording (e.g. grep `approvedAt`, `lock`, `reader`, `stop` scoped to that file) rather than repo-wide `maxReaders|MDB_READERS_FULL` and `SIGTERM|shutdown`. If the issue is not approved, approve it (or note the dependency) before this artifact builds on it; if its wording differs, reword the premise to match.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The file's existence is confirmed, but the approval status and the stated defect content were not captured by the probes (repo-wide greps that returned zero or truncated, unrelated matches). No evidence-derived text correction is possible, so no artifact edits are proposed; a human or a re-run with file-scoped probes must confirm or reword the premise.","options":["Re-run the probes scoped to the ISSUE.md (read the meta/header for `approvedAt` and the problem statement lines) and keep the premise unchanged if both are confirmed.","Narrow the premise to what is verified — the ISSUE.md exists as artifact ISSUE-0d5775698fd042a7 — and cite the approval status and defect statement as separate premises with their own file:line anchors.","If the re-probe shows the issue is not yet approved, approve it first (on the user's explicit yes) or record the unapproved dependency in this artifact before proceeding.","If the re-probe shows the issue words the defect differently (e.g. 'locks' without 'reader slots', or no 'cannot be stopped' claim), reword the premise to quot