as the reviewed artifact's constraints table or section 7, so no evidence-derived text correction exists. The premise needs a re-probe, not an edit.","options":["Re-run both greps restricted to the reviewed artifact's path with the match cap lifted, then re-judge the closure claim against the full match set.","Add `path:line` reads of the artifact's section-5 constraints table and section-7 reference list as anchors so the id sets can be compared directly.","Accept the premise with a note that k-id and citation-marker closure was not independently verified by this review."]}},{"claimId":"p35","ref":"s4, FR006","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"LMDB enforces a system limit on reader/lock slots per environment; when it is exhausted store operations fail, and the daemon's stop path today depends on store operations that then fail, leaving it unable to stop.","evidence":"Unverifiable from the gathered evidence. The probes confirm only that a stop path exists: `src/daemon/index.ts:2031` (`process.on('SIGTERM', () => shutdown('SIGTERM'))`) and `src/daemon/index.ts:2032` (`process.on('SIGINT', () => shutdown('SIGINT'))`), and that the ISSUE artifact exists (`docs/standalone/defect-installed-daemon-runs-out-lmdb-E202610100d577569/ISSUE.md:1`). No probe read the body of `shutdown` or anything in `src/db/graph/store.ts`, so the claim that the stop path depends on store operations that fail when slots are exhausted is not shown. `grep /maxReaders/` and `grep /MDB_READERS_FULL|READERS_FULL/` both returned 0 matches: this shows the repo neither configures the reader limit nor handles the readers-full error by name, which is consistent with the premise but does not establish it. The LMDB reader-slot limit itself is an external library contract that no evidence here addresses.","action":"Before relying on this premise, read the body of `shutdown` in `src/daemon/index.ts` (the handler wired at lines 2031-2032) and record the `file:line` of each store call it awaits, so the dependency of the stop path on store operations is cited rather than asserted. Also cite the source for the LMDB reader/lock slot limit (the lmdb-js `maxReaders` option and the readers-full error) and note that the repo sets no `maxReaders` (0 matches), so the library default applies. Then re-run the review with probes that target those lines.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence neither confirms nor contradicts the premise, so no evidence-derived text correction exists. The premise needs anchors to the specific lines in the stop path and the store that carry the claim; a human should choose how to ground it. No artifact edits are proposed because any replacement text would assert facts the evidence does not contain.","options":["Add `file:line` anchors for the store calls inside `shutdown` in `src/daemon/index.ts` and for the environment open in `src/db/graph/store.ts`, then re-run the probes against those lines.","Split the premise in two: (a) the LMDB reader-slot limit, cited to the lmdb-js documentation as an external contract, and (b) the stop path's dependency on store operations, cited to the code; verify each separately.","Reword the stop-path half as an observed symptom from the defect report (the daemon failed to stop once slots ran out) rather than as a claim about the code's structure, and leave the causal chain to be established during design.","Keep the premise as written and accept it with a note that it is unverified by this review's probes."]}},{"claimId":"p36","ref":"s6/ac1, s6/ac3, k11","kind":"external-contract","severity":"LOW","premise":"Git ignore semantics: a leading-slash (anchored) rule applies only at the level of its own .gitignore, and a .gitignore in a subdirectory applies to that directory and below; `git ls-files --exclude-standard` honours nested .gitignore files in this way.","evidence":"The anchor resolves to the right entity: `read src/indexer/index.ts:120` returns `['ls-files', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z'],`, and the comment at `src/indexer/index.ts:117` reads `--exclude-standard: honour .gitignore, .git/info/exclude, global gitignore`. The same flags are used at `src/analyze/context/repo-ignore-filter.ts:113`, and the recorded suite runs for that filter show both `ok 369 - excludes directories matching a root .gitignore rule` and `ok 371 - respects a subdirectory .gitignore` (e.g. `docs/standalone/bug-plan-tree-analyze-run-when-E202610098ab2cc2e/S001/measurements/analyze-suite-after-f50af845.tap:4745,4757`). So the nested-.gitignore half of the premise is confirmed by a passing test on the same listing command. The leading-slash (anchored) half is not exercised by any gathered match; it is Git's documented behaviour rather than a fact about this repository, and nothing in the evidence contradicts it.","action":"none — verified sound. Optional: because the anchored-rule half rests on Git's documented behaviour and not on a test in this repository, the s6 acceptance tests could include a fixture with a leading-slash rule in a subdirectory .gitignore, so that ac1/ac3 check it directly.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"No correction to the artifact is needed: the cited line is the listing command the premise describes, and the nested-file behaviour is confirmed by a recorded passing test. The only gap is that the anchored-rule behaviour is not covered by a test in this repository, which is a test-coverage suggestion and not an artifact edit."}},{"claimId":"p37","ref":"s6/ac6, k9, s8/ac4","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The daemon has a local (Ollama) model tier distinct from the CLI-backed mid tier, so the ignore-file proposal and subsystem summaries can be routed to the local model while document-relationship extraction goes to the mid tier.","evidence":"Partly confirmed, but the tier naming is not. Both providers exist: `src/agent/providers/ollama.ts:150` declares `export class OllamaProvider implements LLMProvider`, and `src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:131` declares `export class CliProvider implements LLMProvider`. The mid tier is CLI-backed: `docs/daemon.md:381` reads `\"mid\": { \"runner\": \"cli-claude\", \"model\": \"sonnet\" }`. So a local Ollama path distinct from the CLI-backed mid tier is real.\n\nWhat the evidence does not show is a tier named \"local\". The only tier names in the gathered evidence are `cheap`, `mid` and `core` (S005/LLD.md:236 `tierNames [cheap,mid,core]`; `docs/daemon.md:451` `\"coreFloor\": \"core\"`). The `\"local\"` hits at `docs/daemon.md:94` and `:388` are a `models.local` config block (`src/config/local.ts:81` reads `models['local']`; `config-reconcile-smoke.test.ts:98` reads `local.embeddingDim`), a sibling of `tiers`/`tasks`/`coreFloor` in `config-reconcile.test.ts:316`, not a tier. The `'local'` in `src/shared/agent-steps.ts:23` (`StepDefaultTier = 'local' | 'cloud'`) is a separate per-step default, not the per-role tiering at `docs/daemon.md:381`. No evidence line shows which tier's runner is Ollama, so routing the ignore-file proposal and subsystem summaries \"to the local tier\" is not verifiable as worded.\n\nThe prescribed routing (peripheral work to the local model, document-relationship extraction to mid) remains buildable through the existing providers, so this is imprecision rather than a build-breaking defect.","action":"Reword the premise and the s6/ac6, k9 and s8/ac4 references to name the actual tier instead of a \"local tier\". First read the `tiers` block around `docs/daemon.md:375-395` to confirm which tier (expected `cheap`) has the Ollama runner, then state the routing as role-to-tier assignments in `models.tasks`: ignore-file proposal and subsystem summaries to that tier, document-relationship extraction to `mid`. Do not introduce a new tier called `local`, and do not confuse it with the `models.local` config block.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the Ollama provider and the CLI-backed mid tier, but shows the tier names are cheap/mid/core and that `models.local` is a config block rather than a tier. The premise needs rewording to the real tier name; which tier is Ollama-backed must be confirmed from the docs/daemon.md tiers block, which was not in the gathered evidence, so no verbatim artifact edit is safe to emit.","options":["Reword to route the ignore-file proposal and subsystem summaries to the existing Ollama-backed tier (expected `cheap`, after confirming its runner in the docs/daemon.md tiers block) and document-relationship extraction to `mid`, expressed as `models.tasks` role-to-tier entries.","Keep the wording provider-level: say the two peripheral roles are served by OllamaProvider and document-relationship extraction by the CLI-backed `mid` tier, without naming a 'local tier'.","If a tier literally named `local` is intended, re-scope the story to add that tier to the tier model (tier names, catalog, coreFloor ranking) as explicit new work rather than asserting it already exists."]}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":20,"low":17},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-11T05:19:54.318Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
